True, but that is about key length for AES specifically, not security in =
general.  The Suite-B recommendations also have the AES key at 256 bits =
for the higher level but it=92s paired with SHA-384.

Yoav

> On Oct 12, 2015, at 4:18 PM, Sean Turner <sean@sn3rd.com> wrote:
>=20
> It is interesting to note that in discussing update IPSec=92s RFC 4307 =
somebody suggested making 192 a MAY because folks only use 128/256 [1].
>=20
> spt
>=20
> [1] =
http://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ipsec/1F5h4j-dP5dLPCCAqg4iqgjjYFE
>=20
> On Oct 12, 2015, at 05:01, John Mattsson <john.mattsson@ericsson.com> =
wrote:
>=20
>> I think the selection of MTI Cipher Suites (Section 8.1 of =
draft-ietf-tls-tls13-09) is excellent, but I am missing a recommended =
ECC curve for the =93SHOULD=94 cipher suites. Little benefit of using =
AES-256 with P-256 or curve25519. Shouldn=92t there be a SHOULD =
implement ECC curve giving at least 192-bit security? E.g.
>>=20
>> "These cipher suites SHOULD support both digital signatures and key =
exchange with secp384r1 (NIST P-384)."
>>=20
